1
00:00:11,900 --> 00:00:15,200
Chief! It's so hot!

2
00:00:15,200 --> 00:00:16,900
I open the fridge,

3
00:00:16,900 --> 00:00:18,800
my face feels cool!

4
00:00:18,800 --> 00:00:20,367
Great idea!

5
00:00:20,367 --> 00:00:22,127
Leave it open,

6
00:00:22,433 --> 00:00:25,367
and no AC in the kitchen!

7
00:00:25,367 --> 00:00:27,097
Wait, don't unplug!

8
00:00:27,567 --> 00:00:30,627
Guess: fridge door open all day,

9
00:00:31,133 --> 00:00:34,833
kitchen gets cooler, or warmer?

10
00:00:34,900 --> 00:00:36,170
Warmer?

11
00:00:36,233 --> 00:00:38,633
But the fridge is cold!

12
00:00:39,067 --> 00:00:41,597
Next time, hold your hand near its back.

13
00:00:42,833 --> 00:00:45,953
It doesn't feel cold at all.

14
00:00:46,500 --> 00:00:47,920
Behind it,

15
00:00:48,267 --> 00:00:49,627
what's hiding?

16
00:00:50,767 --> 00:00:53,437
And that humming box on your balcony,

17
00:00:54,700 --> 00:00:57,260
does it blow cold air, or hot?

18
00:00:59,300 --> 00:01:00,700
The AC?

19
00:01:00,700 --> 00:01:02,920
Cold air, for sure?

20
00:01:03,900 --> 00:01:06,930
All the answers hide in one fridge.

21
00:01:07,533 --> 00:01:10,823
With its door open,
where does the heat go?

22
00:01:12,333 --> 00:01:15,100
It's full of cold,

23
00:01:15,100 --> 00:01:17,600
open the door, cold comes out!

24
00:01:18,333 --> 00:01:20,963
Ever let hot tea cool down?

25
00:01:21,633 --> 00:01:24,253
Left on the table, what happens?

26
00:01:25,033 --> 00:01:26,583
It gets warm.

27
00:01:26,800 --> 00:01:28,250
Heat left!

28
00:01:28,733 --> 00:01:30,873
And an ice cube?

29
00:01:31,233 --> 00:01:33,293
It slowly melts.

30
00:01:33,333 --> 00:01:36,853
It melts because heat
from the air goes in.

31
00:01:38,533 --> 00:01:42,223
On its own, heat only goes
from hot places to cold.

32
00:01:43,933 --> 00:01:45,600
And cold?

33
00:01:45,600 --> 00:01:48,700
Doesn't cold air move by itself?

34
00:01:48,700 --> 00:01:51,860
What we call cold is less heat.

35
00:01:52,100 --> 00:01:54,930
Cold isn't a thing that leaks out.

36
00:01:55,600 --> 00:01:57,367
Then my face,

37
00:01:57,367 --> 00:01:59,287
why did it feel cool?

38
00:02:00,133 --> 00:02:03,553
Cold air flows out and touches your face,

39
00:02:04,433 --> 00:02:07,963
and your face's heat goes into that air.

40
00:02:08,267 --> 00:02:10,557
My heat got stolen!

41
00:02:11,300 --> 00:02:13,710
To track heat, draw a circle.

42
00:02:14,533 --> 00:02:18,583
Heat in the circle, more or
less, we write it all down.

43
00:02:19,900 --> 00:02:23,400
I'll draw heat as red balls.

44
00:02:23,400 --> 00:02:26,400
First, circle just the inside.

45
00:02:26,400 --> 00:02:29,860
The shelves are only 3 to 5 degrees,

46
00:02:29,967 --> 00:02:32,317
way colder than a summer kitchen.

47
00:02:33,467 --> 00:02:36,300
The freezer? How cold?

48
00:02:36,300 --> 00:02:40,290
Usually minus 18 degrees, or even colder.

49
00:02:40,700 --> 00:02:42,867
Minus 18!

50
00:02:42,867 --> 00:02:45,667
Way colder than winter in Taiwan!

51
00:02:45,667 --> 00:02:48,747
The bigger the gap, the
harder heat pushes in.

52
00:02:49,900 --> 00:02:52,033
Kitchen heat

53
00:02:52,033 --> 00:02:53,973
wants into the fridge?

54
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:59,070
Yes. By that rule, heat
always heads for cold.

55
00:02:59,300 --> 00:03:01,690
But the fridge door gets in its way.

56
00:03:03,133 --> 00:03:05,333
Open it. Feel the edge.

57
00:03:05,733 --> 00:03:09,923
Thick, heavy, with a soft
rubber strip round it.

58
00:03:10,333 --> 00:03:12,593
Thick like a flask!

59
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:16,480
Yes. Door and walls hold insulation.

60
00:03:16,500 --> 00:03:19,440
It's insulation: heat gets in slower.

61
00:03:20,433 --> 00:03:23,313
Remember this thick door. It'll be back.

62
00:03:24,967 --> 00:03:26,533
Just slower?

63
00:03:26,533 --> 00:03:29,200
So heat sneaks in?

64
00:03:29,200 --> 00:03:32,020
Yes. Yet it stays cold,

65
00:03:32,100 --> 00:03:35,140
so something keeps moving heat out.

66
00:03:35,833 --> 00:03:39,083
Heat in the circle goes out, ball by ball.

67
00:03:40,167 --> 00:03:41,387
Heat?

68
00:03:41,500 --> 00:03:43,180
where did it go?

69
00:03:43,867 --> 00:03:46,267
Maybe the fridge

70
00:03:46,267 --> 00:03:47,937
ate the heat?

71
00:03:48,800 --> 00:03:52,080
Heat never vanishes. It just moves.

72
00:03:52,467 --> 00:03:54,507
Not sure? Go round the back.

73
00:03:55,667 --> 00:03:58,167
Huh? It's warm!

74
00:03:58,167 --> 00:04:00,433
A warm breeze!

75
00:04:00,433 --> 00:04:04,200
Just bring your hand
close. There are wires.

76
00:04:04,200 --> 00:04:07,140
These curvy pipes at the back feel warm.

77
00:04:08,300 --> 00:04:11,080
The heat that left the
inside comes out here.

78
00:04:12,767 --> 00:04:15,057
The heat went to the back!

79
00:04:15,700 --> 00:04:18,890
Some fridges blow warm air from below,

80
00:04:19,567 --> 00:04:23,077
Stand in front. Ever felt it on your feet?

81
00:04:23,767 --> 00:04:25,733
Warm feet,

82
00:04:25,733 --> 00:04:27,393
cool face!

83
00:04:27,400 --> 00:04:30,200
But a fridge has no hands.

84
00:04:30,200 --> 00:04:32,150
How does it move heat?

85
00:04:32,567 --> 00:04:35,237
In its pipes, something runs in a loop.

86
00:04:36,533 --> 00:04:37,963
It's called refrigerant.

87
00:04:39,067 --> 00:04:40,100
Huh?

88
00:04:40,100 --> 00:04:42,110
Is it cold itself?

89
00:04:42,467 --> 00:04:46,587
Not always, but it
changes. After a hot bath,

90
00:04:46,900 --> 00:04:49,510
when you step out wet, do you shiver?

91
00:04:50,933 --> 00:04:53,263
Yes! The water was hot!

92
00:04:53,400 --> 00:04:56,650
When the water on you turns into vapor,

93
00:04:57,300 --> 00:04:59,270
it takes your heat with it.

94
00:05:00,433 --> 00:05:03,323
So wet means cold!

95
00:05:03,500 --> 00:05:07,250
In the fridge, refrigerant
turns from liquid to gas,

96
00:05:08,333 --> 00:05:10,783
carrying the heat inside into the pipes.

97
00:05:12,533 --> 00:05:14,733
It's so cold,

98
00:05:14,733 --> 00:05:17,900
and it can still turn to gas?

99
00:05:17,900 --> 00:05:21,730
Yes. It's a special
liquid that boils easily,

100
00:05:22,300 --> 00:05:24,830
so a little heat in the fridge is enough.

101
00:05:26,300 --> 00:05:28,733
Boiling, but cold?

102
00:05:28,733 --> 00:05:31,063
What a weird liquid!

103
00:05:31,467 --> 00:05:32,967
The hot gas,

104
00:05:33,233 --> 00:05:34,773
where next?

105
00:05:35,600 --> 00:05:37,640
Into a round metal can.

106
00:05:38,400 --> 00:05:40,770
That can is the compressor.

107
00:05:41,667 --> 00:05:44,597
It squeezes the gas tight.

108
00:05:44,733 --> 00:05:48,273
The squeezed refrigerant
flows into the back pipes,

109
00:05:49,600 --> 00:05:52,370
turns liquid, giving heat to the kitchen.

110
00:05:53,633 --> 00:05:56,813
Then it flows back inside
and goes round again.

111
00:05:58,267 --> 00:06:00,827
A little train carrying heat!

112
00:06:01,333 --> 00:06:05,733
Pretty much. Round and round,
carrying heat from in to out.

113
00:06:07,167 --> 00:06:09,127
Who pushes the train?

114
00:06:10,667 --> 00:06:14,687
Electricity does.
Squeezing gas is hard work,

115
00:06:15,067 --> 00:06:17,837
so the compressor keeps using power.

116
00:06:18,633 --> 00:06:22,153
We'll draw electricity as a yellow bolt.

117
00:06:22,767 --> 00:06:26,147
It's not nonstop. When
it warms, it starts,

118
00:06:26,967 --> 00:06:29,233
when cold, it rests.

119
00:06:29,233 --> 00:06:31,603
That's why it's quiet sometimes!

120
00:06:32,667 --> 00:06:34,833
The power it uses,

121
00:06:34,833 --> 00:06:36,463
where does it end up?

122
00:06:37,133 --> 00:06:39,263
And the heat at the back,

123
00:06:39,767 --> 00:06:41,633
and from inside

124
00:06:41,633 --> 00:06:43,413
are they equal?

125
00:06:43,667 --> 00:06:46,677
To answer, draw a bigger circle,

126
00:06:46,933 --> 00:06:48,453
around the whole kitchen.

127
00:06:49,900 --> 00:06:53,680
The fridge, its back, where you stand,

128
00:06:53,867 --> 00:06:55,977
are all in one circle now.

129
00:06:58,333 --> 00:07:00,903
Door shut. Let's use easy numbers:

130
00:07:01,700 --> 00:07:04,780
the fridge moves out two balls of heat,

131
00:07:05,433 --> 00:07:07,913
and the compressor uses one bolt.

132
00:07:08,767 --> 00:07:11,087
How many out the back?

133
00:07:11,133 --> 00:07:14,513
Three. The two moved out, plus one.

134
00:07:14,633 --> 00:07:17,093
That one comes from
the compressor's power.

135
00:07:18,733 --> 00:07:21,633
More comes out than was moved out!

136
00:07:22,133 --> 00:07:26,223
Yes. Used power doesn't
vanish. It ends up as heat.

137
00:07:27,433 --> 00:07:30,443
Door shut, does the kitchen warm up?

138
00:07:31,333 --> 00:07:35,623
Those two balls sneaked in
slowly through walls and door.

139
00:07:36,733 --> 00:07:40,403
Now they're back, plus one from power.

140
00:07:41,267 --> 00:07:44,033
So even with it shut,

141
00:07:44,033 --> 00:07:46,523
the kitchen warms a bit?

142
00:07:46,700 --> 00:07:50,420
Yes. While the fridge runs,
it warms the kitchen a little.

143
00:07:52,067 --> 00:07:54,100
If I open it,

144
00:07:54,100 --> 00:07:55,170
then what?

145
00:07:55,633 --> 00:07:58,123
Kitchen heat pours in the door,

146
00:07:59,133 --> 00:08:01,613
and goes out the back, to the kitchen.

147
00:08:02,900 --> 00:08:05,180
Heat goes in circles!

148
00:08:06,300 --> 00:08:09,630
Yes. The two it moved out run back in.

149
00:08:10,567 --> 00:08:13,637
The kitchen doesn't lose a single one.

150
00:08:14,433 --> 00:08:16,743
But each loop, the bolt

151
00:08:17,300 --> 00:08:19,880
adds one more ball of heat.

152
00:08:20,433 --> 00:08:24,333
So with the door open,

153
00:08:24,333 --> 00:08:26,433
the kitchen gets warmer!

154
00:08:26,433 --> 00:08:28,503
But at the door,

155
00:08:28,600 --> 00:08:30,900
it really is cool!

156
00:08:30,900 --> 00:08:33,260
True. Right at the door, it's cooler.

157
00:08:34,767 --> 00:08:38,717
But walk away, and that
cool spot isn't yours.

158
00:08:39,033 --> 00:08:41,983
Open, the inside keeps warming,

159
00:08:42,267 --> 00:08:45,237
so the compressor can't stop using power.

160
00:08:46,933 --> 00:08:49,203
Poor tired fridge!

161
00:08:49,733 --> 00:08:51,643
Your power meter too,

162
00:08:52,367 --> 00:08:54,057
and the bill goes up.

163
00:08:55,133 --> 00:08:58,300
A fridge with its door open

164
00:08:58,300 --> 00:09:00,420
is like a winter heater?

165
00:09:01,033 --> 00:09:03,223
Yes. Just the long way,

166
00:09:03,767 --> 00:09:05,907
and warms the food inside too.

167
00:09:07,767 --> 00:09:09,627
The fridge's big trick:

168
00:09:10,500 --> 00:09:13,120
it moves heat. It doesn't make cold.

169
00:09:15,100 --> 00:09:16,890
It fooled me!

170
00:09:17,167 --> 00:09:19,233
And the AC?

171
00:09:19,233 --> 00:09:21,803
Does the AC move heat too?

172
00:09:22,467 --> 00:09:25,087
Yes. They're the same kind of machine.

173
00:09:26,267 --> 00:09:30,367
Refrigerant, compressor,
and it uses power.

174
00:09:30,667 --> 00:09:33,367
So with AC on,

175
00:09:33,367 --> 00:09:35,557
how does the room get cool?

176
00:09:36,067 --> 00:09:39,207
The answer's on the
balcony. Let's go look.

177
00:09:40,400 --> 00:09:43,633
Wow, it's blowing hot air!

178
00:09:43,633 --> 00:09:46,943
This is the outdoor unit. Its heat

179
00:09:47,333 --> 00:09:48,953
was moved out of your room.

180
00:09:51,200 --> 00:09:54,320
I know! It takes outside air,

181
00:09:54,333 --> 00:09:56,883
cools it and blows it in!

182
00:09:56,967 --> 00:10:00,207
Lots of people think so. But the AC blows

183
00:10:01,267 --> 00:10:03,017
your room's own air.

184
00:10:05,067 --> 00:10:08,700
The wall unit sucks air in,
takes heat, blows it back.

185
00:10:08,700 --> 00:10:14,667
It's joined to the
balcony one by two pipes.

186
00:10:14,667 --> 00:10:17,187
Refrigerant loops between,

187
00:10:17,467 --> 00:10:19,017
sound like the fridge?

188
00:10:20,533 --> 00:10:22,053
The heat train!

189
00:10:22,633 --> 00:10:27,193
Now circle your room: the
room is the fridge's inside,

190
00:10:28,500 --> 00:10:30,670
and the fridge's back goes outside.

191
00:10:32,167 --> 00:10:34,367
The hot end is outside!

192
00:10:34,733 --> 00:10:38,093
This time the heat goes
outside and doesn't loop back,

193
00:10:39,800 --> 00:10:43,300
so the heat in the
circle really goes down.

194
00:10:43,900 --> 00:10:46,660
Most of its power becomes heat,

195
00:10:47,200 --> 00:10:49,300
and goes out the outdoor unit.

196
00:10:50,367 --> 00:10:53,200
A normal AC, 1 bolt,

197
00:10:53,200 --> 00:10:55,667
moves several balls of heat.

198
00:10:55,667 --> 00:10:58,767
One bolt moves several balls?

199
00:10:58,767 --> 00:11:00,577
That's a lot!

200
00:11:01,100 --> 00:11:03,750
It depends on the AC and weather.

201
00:11:04,333 --> 00:11:06,693
The hotter outside, the harder.

202
00:11:07,800 --> 00:11:09,980
Recall the thick door?

203
00:11:10,267 --> 00:11:14,317
With the AC on, you shut
windows, close curtains,

204
00:11:14,800 --> 00:11:16,590
a thick door for it.

205
00:11:17,267 --> 00:11:20,087
So that's why we shut windows!

206
00:11:20,400 --> 00:11:22,740
The AC moves heat out,

207
00:11:23,267 --> 00:11:26,927
insulation keeps outside
heat from sneaking back fast.

208
00:11:28,267 --> 00:11:30,667
One moves, one blocks!

209
00:11:30,667 --> 00:11:32,987
AC on, windows open?

210
00:11:33,567 --> 00:11:37,077
Like an open fridge
door. It works nonstop.

211
00:11:37,867 --> 00:11:41,067
An AC room is

212
00:11:41,067 --> 00:11:43,333
a big fridge!

213
00:11:43,333 --> 00:11:45,300
Yes, and you live in it.

214
00:11:45,300 --> 00:11:49,740
OK, back to the kitchen.
The door's still open.

215
00:11:49,800 --> 00:11:51,560
Remember my idea?

216
00:11:51,800 --> 00:11:53,860
I take it back!

217
00:11:54,533 --> 00:11:56,973
Door open, the hot end

218
00:11:57,200 --> 00:11:59,190
stays in the kitchen!

219
00:11:59,600 --> 00:12:02,610
Fridge and AC: same machine, remember?

220
00:12:03,367 --> 00:12:07,900
The difference: hot end
in the circle or out?

221
00:12:07,900 --> 00:12:10,250
Next time, feel its back:

222
00:12:11,100 --> 00:12:15,410
remember those three red balls?
That warmth you feel is them.

223
00:12:16,500 --> 00:12:18,500
So that's why!

224
00:12:18,600 --> 00:12:20,930
Where is your AC unit?

225
00:12:21,100 --> 00:12:23,700
How hot is its air? Tell us below.

226
00:12:24,967 --> 00:12:29,897
Find life's everyday whys with
us. Subscribe to So That's Why.
